msgid "HTTP rate limit reference guide"
msgstr ""

msgid "This technology is considered {extension-status}."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In _experimental_ mode, early feedback is requested to mature the idea.\n"
"There is no guarantee of stability nor long term presence in the platform "
"until the solution matures.\n"
"Feedback is welcome on our https://groups.google.com/d/forum/quarkus-"
"dev[mailing list] or as issues in our https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/"
"issues[GitHub issue tracker]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a full list of possible statuses, check our https://quarkus.io/faq/#what-"
"are-the-extension-statuses[FAQ entry]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Rate limiting controls how frequently a client can access an application.\n"
"It stops a single caller from using more than its share: a client stuck in a "
"retry loop, someone guessing passwords on a login page, or a customer that "
"goes beyond its plan."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-http-rate-limit` extension enforces rate limits in the Quarkus "
"HTTP layer, before requests reach application endpoints.\n"
"You configure named rules that select HTTP paths and methods, identify which "
"requests share a limit, and define the permitted request rate.\n"
"Requests that exceed a limit receive `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-"
"After` header.\n"
"The extension rejects excess requests immediately; it does not queue them "
"until capacity becomes available."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Start with <<getting-started>> to configure and try one rule.\n"
"The next sections explain that rule, how its permits replenish, and how to "
"count requests by client.\n"
"Then use <<common-tasks>> to adapt it to your application."
msgstr ""

msgid "Getting started"
msgstr ""

msgid "Add the extension to your project:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The extension limits nothing until you configure a _rule_.\n"
"The following rule, named `api`, lets each client IP address make 100 "
"requests per minute to the paths under `/api`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`paths` are the paths the rule limits."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`request-key` says who counts as one client, here the client IP address. See "
"<<keys>> for the other choices."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`phase` says whether the rule runs before or after the user is authenticated."
" A rule keyed by IP address needs no user, so it runs before. See <<phases>>."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`permits` and `window` are the limit: 100 requests per minute. See "
"<<choosing-the-limit>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The rule name, `api` here, is your choice.\n"
"It appears in logs and metrics, never in responses."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To see the limit at work, provide an endpoint at `/api/hello` and start the "
"application in dev mode with a smaller limit: `permits=2` and `window=1m`.\n"
"Send three requests in quick succession from the same client:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The first two get the normal response.\n"
"The third is refused, with a response like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`Retry-After` is the number of seconds until the client may try again.\n"
"In dev mode, the application log also shows every refusal, with the rule and "
"the key that was refused."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To disable rate limiting when starting the application, set `quarkus.http."
"rate-limit.enabled=false`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Understanding your first rule"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The five properties in <<getting-started>> define one complete rule named "
"`api`.\n"
"Read them as three decisions:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Decision"
msgstr ""

msgid "In this example"
msgstr ""

msgid "Which requests are limited?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`paths=/api/*` selects requests under `/api`.\n"
"Requests to other paths are unaffected by this rule."
msgstr ""

msgid "Which requests count together?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`request-key=ip` groups requests from the same IP address.\n"
"`phase=pre-authentication` lets the rule run before a user is identified, "
"because it only needs the client address."
msgstr ""

msgid "How many requests are allowed?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`permits=100` and `window=1m` give each client IP address a rate of 100 "
"requests per minute."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Requests from one IP address to `/api/hello` and `/api/orders` use the same "
"allowance.\n"
"Another IP address has its own allowance.\n"
"All clients get the same permitted rate, but each uses its allowance "
"independently."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In `quarkus.http.rate-limit.rules.api.paths`, `rules` holds the configured "
"rules, `api` is the name you chose, and `paths` is one setting of that rule.\n"
"To protect another set of paths separately, add a rule with a different name."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The next section explains how the allowance replenishes over time.\n"
"Then <<keys>> explains how to count by authenticated user or another value "
"instead of IP address."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choosing the limit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each allowed request consumes one _permit_ from the client's allowance.\n"
"Used permits replenish over time, so the client can make more requests later."
"\n"
"The allowance is not reset at fixed times.\n"
"Permits come back one at a time, evenly: with `permits=60` and `window=1m`, "
"a client gets one permit back every second, not 60 at the top of each minute."
"\n"
"That time between two permits, `window / permits`, is the rule's _interval_."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`burst` says how many permits a client can save up while it is idle, and so "
"how many requests it can make at once.\n"
"It defaults to `permits`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following table follows one client of a rule with `permits=2` and "
"`window=10s`, so one permit back every 5 seconds, for three values of "
"`burst`.\n"
"The client starts idle, with as many permits as it can hold:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Time"
msgstr ""

msgid "Requests"
msgstr ""

msgid "`burst=1`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`burst=2`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`burst=4`"
msgstr ""

msgid "0 s"
msgstr ""

msgid "5 at once"
msgstr ""

msgid "1 admitted, 4 refused with `Retry-After: 5`"
msgstr ""

msgid "2 admitted, 3 refused with `Retry-After: 5`"
msgstr ""

msgid "4 admitted, 1 refused with `Retry-After: 5`"
msgstr ""

msgid "2 s"
msgstr ""

msgid "1"
msgstr ""

msgid "refused with `Retry-After: 3`"
msgstr ""

msgid "5 s"
msgstr ""

msgid "admitted"
msgstr ""

msgid "10 s"
msgstr ""

msgid "30 s"
msgstr ""

msgid "3 at once"
msgstr ""

msgid "1 admitted, 2 refused with `Retry-After: 5`"
msgstr ""

msgid "2 admitted, 1 refused with `Retry-After: 5`"
msgstr ""

msgid "3 admitted"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In all three columns, the client gets one request through every 5 seconds "
"once its saved permits are spent.\n"
"`burst` only changes how many it can save: after the quiet 20 seconds, it "
"has 1, 2 or 4."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Raise `burst` above `permits` to allow short spikes from clients that are "
"mostly idle."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Lower it below `permits` to spread each client's requests out more evenly."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`Retry-After` is the wait until the next permit comes back, in whole "
"seconds, at least 1.\n"
"A client that retries exactly then may still be refused, if another request "
"with the same key used that permit first."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choosing what to count"
msgstr ""

msgid "The `request-key` of a rule decides which requests share one limit:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Key"
msgstr ""

msgid "Requests that share a limit"
msgstr ""

msgid "`ip`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Requests from the same client IP address."
msgstr ""

msgid "`principal`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Requests of the same authenticated user.\n"
"Requests without a user are counted by IP address."
msgstr ""

msgid "`header:<name>`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Requests with the same value of the header `<name>`, for example `header:X-"
"Api-Key`.\n"
"Requests without the header, or with a blank value, are counted by IP "
"address."
msgstr ""

msgid "`global`"
msgstr ""

msgid "All requests of the rule, from any client."
msgstr ""

msgid "`custom:<name>`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Requests that your own key resolver gives the same key, see <<custom-keys>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Keys are scoped to a rule and phase: the same client has independent limits "
"in two different rules.\n"
"Within one rule, requests with the same key share a limit across all of its "
"paths and methods.\n"
"For example, an `ip` rule with `paths=/orders/*,/search/*` gives each "
"address one combined allowance for both sets of paths.\n"
"With `global`, all matching requests share one limit.\n"
"For example, a rule with `paths=/items/*` and `request-key=global` counts "
"requests to `/items/1` and `/items/2` together, from every client.\n"
"One busy client can exhaust that allowance for everyone."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before or after authentication"
msgstr ""

msgid "Each rule runs in one of two phases, set with `phase`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`pre-authentication` runs before the user is known.\n"
"Use it for keys that need no user, such as `ip`.\n"
"It also protects authentication itself: a login request with wrong "
"credentials is answered `401` by authentication and never reaches a later "
"phase, so limit password guessing with a `pre-authentication` rule keyed by "
"`ip`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`post-authentication` runs once the user is known, before the permission "
"checks.\n"
"`principal` keys need it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To limit the same paths in both phases, for example by IP address before "
"authentication and by user after it, write one rule for each."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A header key in the `pre-authentication` phase counts values that nothing "
"has checked yet.\n"
"Use it only for values that are secret, such as API keys, together with an "
"`ip` rule on the same paths.\n"
"For a value that is not secret, such as a tenant ID, validate that the "
"authenticated caller may use that value before treating it as a trusted key.\n"
"Selecting `post-authentication` makes the identity available but does not "
"validate a header's relationship to that identity.\n"
"A custom resolver can derive the tenant from the identity instead."
msgstr ""

msgid "Behind a proxy"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `ip` key uses the address that the request comes from.\n"
"Behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, that is the proxy's address, and "
"all clients share one limit.\n"
"Set `quarkus.http.proxy.proxy-address-forwarding=true` and `quarkus.http."
"proxy.trusted-proxies` so that Quarkus takes the client's address from the "
"proxy's forwarding headers, see xref:http-reference.adoc#reverse-"
"proxy[Running behind a reverse proxy]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Common tasks"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The examples below build on the rule from <<getting-started>>.\n"
"Start with the task you need: restrict HTTP methods, combine rules, or reuse "
"settings.\n"
"The later examples introduce named quotas and Java code for choosing a quota "
"by user."
msgstr ""

msgid "Protecting a login page"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The first rule applies to every HTTP method on its paths.\n"
"To limit only login submissions, add `methods=POST` to a rule for `/login`.\n"
"It counts attempts by IP address before authentication:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Combining a short and a long limit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To allow a few requests per second but also cap the requests per day, write "
"two rules on the same paths:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Within each phase, all matching rules apply, including overlapping paths; a "
"more specific path does not replace a broader rule.\n"
"In this example, the 10-second rule is checked before the daily rule.\n"
"The first refusal stops evaluation and answers the request.\n"
"A request that the short rule admits and the long rule refuses still uses a "
"permit of the short rule."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sharing settings between rules"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When several rules use the same `phase` and `request-key`, set those values "
"once under `quarkus.http.rate-limit`.\n"
"Each rule inherits them unless it sets its own value.\n"
"Keep each rule's paths and permitted rate on the rule.\n"
"Here both rules count requests by IP address before authentication:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Reusing a configured limit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If several rules need the same permitted rate, you can also give that quota "
"a name and reuse it.\n"
"A _named limit_ holds `permits`, `window`, and optionally `burst` under "
"`quarkus.http.rate-limit.limits.<name>`.\n"
"Defining one alone does not limit any requests: a rule selects it with "
"`limit=<name>`.\n"
"For example, define a quota named `standard` and select it from two rules:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each IP address can make 100 requests per minute to `/orders/*` and another "
"100 to `/search/*`.\n"
"The rules share the quota values, not the consumed permits.\n"
"Changing `limits.standard.permits` changes the rate for both rules on the "
"next application startup."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`standard` is an arbitrary quota name; `orders` and `search` are independent "
"rule names.\n"
"The rules still choose which paths to protect and which requests count "
"together.\n"
"Selecting it with `limit=standard` replaces the rule's inline `permits`, "
"`window`, and `burst`; do not configure both.\n"
"No Java code is needed for this use case."
msgstr ""

msgid "Giving users different limits"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"So far, every client of a rule gets the same permitted rate.\n"
"For subscription plans, each user might need a different rate on the same "
"paths.\n"
"A _limit resolver_ is application code that chooses a quota for each request."
"\n"
"Implement `RateLimitResolver` and select it on the rule with `limit-"
"resolver`.\n"
"The following example defines two named quotas, `free` and `paid`, and uses "
"a resolver named `subscription` to choose between them.\n"
"Each free user gets 100 requests a minute and each paid user gets 1000:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`limit-resolver=subscription` selects the CDI bean annotated with "
"`@Identifier(\"subscription\")`.\n"
"That bean selects a limit using the authenticated user's roles:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Configure authentication separately so that the user's identity and roles "
"are available.\n"
"The names `free` and `paid` have no built-in meaning: the Java code "
"explicitly maps the `paid` role to `limits.paid`, and all other users to "
"`limits.free`.\n"
"An anonymous request gets the free limit and is counted by its client "
"address.\n"
"Use an `authenticated` HTTP permission if anonymous access should be refused."
"\n"
"Each user has an independent allowance, even when several users select the "
"same named limit.\n"
"The `request-key` decides who shares a counter; the selected limit decides "
"its size and replenishment rate."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`RateLimits` is injectable application-wide and loads named limits at "
"startup.\n"
"`get(name)` returns an immutable `RateLimit`; an unknown name throws "
"`IllegalArgumentException`.\n"
"For quotas obtained from application data, a resolver can return `RateLimit."
"of(permits, window)` or\n"
"`RateLimit.of(permits, window, burst)` instead.\n"
"The factories validate their arguments immediately; the two-argument factory "
"defaults the burst to the permits.\n"
"Resolvers run on the event loop and must not block; use a reactive client "
"for subscription lookups.\n"
"Use `context.identity()` for the authenticated user rather than parsing "
"storage keys."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A rule must select exactly one source of limits.\n"
"A static `limit` or inline limit takes precedence over the shared `quarkus."
"http.rate-limit.limit-resolver`.\n"
"An explicit rule-level `limit-resolver` cannot be combined with a static "
"limit."
msgstr ""

msgid "Handling a failed limit lookup"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A resolver that fails, emits `null`, or exceeds `quarkus.http.rate-limit."
"limit-resolver-timeout` (default `250ms`)\n"
"causes a `503 Service Unavailable` response.\n"
"To use a configured limit during a lookup failure instead, select a named "
"fallback.\n"
"For example, add this property to the <<limits,subscription example>> to use "
"its `free` quota if the resolver fails:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The fallback is used only on failure; the resolver selects the normal limit "
"for every request.\n"
"No fallback is required to use a resolver."
msgstr ""

msgid "Changing a user&#8217;s limit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each rule resolves its own limit. Selecting a different named limit keeps "
"the counter for the same rule and request key."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The limit is a token bucket, implemented with the Generic Cell Rate "
"Algorithm (GCRA), which keeps the counts of a key as time, not as a number "
"of used permits: a key that used up its limit owes the time it takes to earn "
"its permits back.\n"
"So raising `permits` without widening `window` does not unlock a key at once."
"\n"
"A key that used up 5 requests a minute and is raised to 10 a minute is still "
"refused for 6 seconds, one interval of the new limit; raised to 10 per 2 "
"minutes instead, it is admitted at once.\n"
"A key never owes more than its current burst: a key that moves from 1 "
"request a day to 100 a minute waits at most one interval, not the rest of "
"the day.\n"
"The same applies when a rolling update changes `permits` or `window`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A rate limit must allow at least one permit; use authorization to deny "
"access.\n"
"Make a resolved limit depend on trusted user or tenant data, not on other "
"request data that the client controls.\n"
"If you use <<lease-mode>>, a key whose limit changes loses the permits its "
"instance had leased."
msgstr ""

msgid "Running several instances"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, each instance of the application keeps its own counts in memory: "
"the `local` store.\n"
"That needs no setup, but each instance applies the limit on its own.\n"
"On three instances behind a load balancer, a limit of 100 requests a minute "
"lets a client make up to 300.\n"
"A restart also forgets every count, and all clients start again with a full "
"burst."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To share one limit across all instances, keep the counts in Redis instead, "
"with the `redis` store.\n"
"A rule uses one store or the other, never both."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using Redis"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Add the `quarkus-redis-client` extension, and select the `redis` store for "
"every rule, or with `store` for some rules only:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Redis 5 or later is needed, standalone or cluster.\n"
"The time used for every decision is that of the Redis server, so the clocks "
"of the instances don't matter."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Give the rate limiter its own Redis client, so that it does not compete with "
"the rest of the application for connections, and size it for the limiter:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`max-pool-size`: about the request rate times the round trip to Redis, plus "
"headroom.\n"
"For example, 2000 requests per second at a round trip of 5 ms keep about 10 "
"connections busy.\n"
"Every instance opens its own pool."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`max-pool-waiting=0`: when every connection is busy, a request is decided at "
"once, by the failure mode below, instead of waiting for one."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If several applications share one Redis database, give each its own `quarkus."
"application.name`, which keeps their keys apart."
msgstr ""

msgid "When Redis is unavailable"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A call to Redis that fails, or takes longer than `quarkus.http.rate-limit."
"store-timeout` (`100ms` by default), is decided by the failure mode, "
"`quarkus.http.rate-limit.store-failure-mode`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Mode"
msgstr ""

msgid "Behaviour"
msgstr ""

msgid "`local` (default)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The instance decides alone, from its own counts in memory, until Redis is "
"back.\n"
"The limit is then per instance."
msgstr ""

msgid "`open`"
msgstr ""

msgid "The request is admitted."
msgstr ""

msgid "`closed`"
msgstr ""

msgid "The request is refused with `503 Service Unavailable`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose `closed` when requests must be rejected if the shared store cannot "
"decide, `open` when availability matters more than enforcing the limit "
"during an outage, or `local` to keep enforcing a per-instance limit.\n"
"These modes govern store failures; they do not remove the precision "
"tradeoffs of lease mode or Redis failover."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"After repeated failures, the extension stops calling Redis for a few "
"seconds, then tries again; while it waits, every request is decided by the "
"failure mode.\n"
"A WARN message is logged when that starts, and an INFO message when Redis is "
"used again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Redis client extension adds a readiness check that fails while Redis is "
"down, which takes every instance out of service whatever the failure mode.\n"
"If the application uses Redis only for rate limiting, disable that check "
"with `quarkus.redis.health.enabled=false`, so that the failure mode decides "
"instead."
msgstr ""

msgid "Fewer calls to Redis: lease mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With the `redis` store, every request makes one call to Redis.\n"
"For limits with many requests per key, lease mode lets each instance take "
"several permits at once and hand them out locally:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The price is precision: the instances together may admit up to `lease-size` "
"requests more than the limit.\n"
"Keep `lease-size` small next to the limit, at most `permits / 20`; the "
"extension warns otherwise.\n"
"Lease mode does not help when most requests come with a key that the "
"instance has not seen recently."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, a request that finds no permit at hand waits for one call to "
"Redis.\n"
"With `max-overdraft=k`, an instance instead admits up to `k` such requests "
"without waiting, and pays for them with its next call; if Redis refuses, "
"they got through over the limit.\n"
"Use it for limits where waiting costs more than an occasional extra request."
msgstr ""

msgid "Lease guarantees and tradeoffs"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A claim takes between 1 and `lease-size` permits from the store, depending "
"on how many the instance used recently, and never more than half of what the "
"store said was left.\n"
"The number of instances never needs to be configured.\n"
"An idle instance holds no permits, and a stopped instance loses at most the "
"permits it held."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Leased permits may be handed out for a _lease time_ of `lease-size` "
"intervals (`window / permits`) from the claim, then expire.\n"
"For example, with `permits=600` and `window=1m`, the interval is 100 ms, so "
"`lease-size=30` gives a lease time of 3 seconds.\n"
"For a key whose resolved limit has a burst below `lease-size`, that burst "
"takes the place of `lease-size`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Permits taken from the store: over any period, the instances together admit "
"at most the limit plus `lease-size`, whatever the number of instances, "
"restarts or delays."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With `max-overdraft=k`: each instance may also admit up to `k` requests per "
"key that no claim pays for, in each interval, or in each `Retry-After` of "
"the store's last refusal if that is longer, plus `k` more after a restart, "
"after the key was forgotten, or after the key's limit changed.\n"
"This grows with the number of instances: it is the price of never waiting.\n"
"`max-overdraft` is at most the key's burst, and needs `lease-size`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"While the store is unavailable, permits granted by the `local` and `open` "
"failure modes are per instance and not part of these bounds.\n"
"Permits already leased keep being used during an outage, even with `closed`, "
"which only refuses new claims."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Leased permits that expire, or whose claim reply was lost, are used up "
"without being handed out: see `http.rate.limit.lease.stranded`.\n"
"Repeated lost replies on one instance can use up a key's whole limit."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"After a refusal, the instance makes no new claim for the key until the "
"store's `Retry-After` has passed."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A request waits for a claim at most `store-timeout`; then the failure mode "
"decides."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For example, take a limit of 120 requests per hour per API key on 3 "
"instances.\n"
"The interval is `3600 s / 120 = 30 s`.\n"
"With `lease-size=6` and `max-overdraft=1`, the lease time is 3 minutes, the "
"instances admit at most 6 requests more than the limit, plus at most one "
"request per instance every 30 seconds while every claim is refused, and "
"Redis sees one call per up to 6 requests."
msgstr ""

msgid "Operating Redis"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following details explain how Redis stores counters and how the "
"extension handles slow or failed store calls."
msgstr ""

msgid "Key storage, expiry, and failover"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Keys in Redis start with `quarkus.http.rate-limit.redis.key-prefix`, by "
"default `quarkus:http-rate-limit:<application name>:`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`max-keys` bounds the memory of an instance, not that of Redis.\n"
"A key stays in Redis until its burst is full again.\n"
"Set a `maxmemory` limit with the `volatile-ttl` policy: every key has an "
"expiry, and those closest to it go first.\n"
"Under `noeviction`, a full Redis fails every call, and the failure mode "
"decides every request."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With asynchronous replication, a Redis failover can lose the latest updates, "
"so keys start again from an older state: the limit errs lenient."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"While Redis refuses a key, the instance refuses the key's requests until the "
"`Retry-After` has passed without calling Redis, so a client over its limit "
"costs Redis nothing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`quarkus.http.rate-limit.redis.timeout` and `quarkus.http.rate-limit.redis."
"failure-mode` override `store-timeout` and `store-failure-mode` for the "
"`redis` store."
msgstr ""

msgid "Store timeouts"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A call to a non-local store has a time budget of `quarkus.http.rate-limit."
"store-timeout` (`100ms` by default).\n"
"For Redis, `quarkus.http.rate-limit.redis.timeout` overrides that value."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A call to Redis that times out may still be carried out by Redis: commands "
"already sent, or waiting for a connection, still run.\n"
"After Redis hangs, up to `max-pool-size` plus `max-pool-waiting` permits can "
"be used up for requests that the failure mode already decided, so the limit "
"errs strict.\n"
"A long pause of the application, such as a garbage collection, can also make "
"calls time out while Redis is healthy; with the `closed` failure mode, keep "
"`store-timeout` well above such pauses."
msgstr ""

msgid "Concurrency limits and circuit breaker"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Calls to a store other than `local` are protected by the following values:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"At most `256` calls to the same store are in flight at once; a call beyond "
"that fails at once."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A circuit breaker looks at the last `20` calls, opens when half of them "
"failed, and stays open for `5s`.\n"
"Then one trial call is made: a success closes it, a failure opens it again.\n"
"The trial is made as soon as the delay ends, without waiting for a request."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A call refused because the Redis connection pool is full falls back to the "
"failure mode, but does not open the breaker: it means the pool is too small, "
"not that Redis is down."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The calls that failed, timeouts included, are counted by the `ft."
"circuitbreaker.*` meters.\n"
"The circuit breaker can be tuned with a customizer, see <<customizers>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "Observability"
msgstr ""

msgid "Metrics"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With the `quarkus-micrometer` extension, the following meters are published.\n"
"Set `quarkus.http.rate-limit.metrics.enabled=false` to turn them off.\n"
"Meters describe rule evaluations: a request admitted by one rule and "
"rejected by another contributes to both rules.\n"
"Rules that are skipped or never reached do not count as admitted requests.\n"
"Keys and client addresses are not metric tags."
msgstr ""

msgid "Meter"
msgstr ""

msgid "Type"
msgstr ""

msgid "Tags and meaning"
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.requests`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Counter"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`, `result`.\n"
"`result` is `allowed` or `rejected`, and, for rules on Redis, `unavailable` "
"for the `503` answers of the `closed` failure mode."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.near.limit`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`.\n"
"Admitted requests that left the key less than a fifth of its burst."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.retry.after`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Distribution summary"
msgstr ""

msgid "`rule`, `phase`.\n"
"The `Retry-After`, in seconds, of the refusals."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.keys`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Gauge"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`.\n"
"The number of keys held in this instance's memory, for rules on the `local` "
"store and lease rules."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.lease.claims`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`, `result` (`granted` or `refused`).\n"
"Lease rules only: the calls that took permits from Redis."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.lease.stranded`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`.\n"
"Permits taken from Redis that this instance never handed out."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.lease.overdraft`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`, `result`.\n"
"Requests that a lease rule with `max-overdraft` admitted without waiting, by "
"what became of them: see <<overdraft-results>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.lease.overdraft.unreconciled`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`.\n"
"Requests admitted on overdraft whose payment is still unknown."
msgstr ""

msgid "`http.rate.limit.lease.fallback.discarded`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`rule`, `phase`.\n"
"Permits granted locally while Redis was unavailable and dropped unused."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The health of the Redis store is visible through the `ft.circuitbreaker.*` "
"meters of SmallRye Fault Tolerance, tagged `http-rate-limit-store-redis`.\n"
"See xref:telemetry-micrometer.adoc[Micrometer] for how to expose meters."
msgstr ""

msgid "Interpreting overdraft results"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`http.rate.limit.lease.overdraft` counts each request admitted on overdraft "
"once as `admitted`, and once more under one of the other results once it is "
"known how it was paid for:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`result`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

msgid "`admitted`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Admitted on overdraft, before any claim paid for it."
msgstr ""

msgid "`settled`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Paid for by the next claim the store granted."
msgstr ""

msgid "`settled_fallback`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Paid for by the local counts, while the store was unavailable and `store-"
"failure-mode` is `local`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`settled_late`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Paid for by a claim whose answer arrived after the instance had lost track "
"of the request."
msgstr ""

msgid "`forgiven`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Never paid for: the store refused the claim, the store was unavailable under "
"`open` or `closed`, or the instance forgot the key before any claim carried "
"the request.\n"
"The request got through over the limit."
msgstr ""

msgid "`unreconciled`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The instance lost track of the request, because the key was forgotten or its "
"limit changed, before the store answered the claim that carries it.\n"
"It is counted again as `settled_late`, `settled_fallback` or `forgiven` when "
"the answer arrives."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"These counts do not measure by how much a limit was exceeded: `forgiven` "
"counts only the requests known to be unpaid."
msgstr ""

msgid "JFR events"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With the `quarkus-jfr` extension, two JDK Flight Recorder events are "
"recorded, in the `Quarkus` / `HTTP` category:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.RateLimitRejected`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One for every `429`, with the rule, the phase, the HTTP method, the path, "
"the `Retry-After`, and the trace and span IDs of the request."
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.RateLimitEvaluation`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One for each rule checked for a request, lasting from the start of the check "
"to its outcome: `ALLOWED`, `REJECTED`, `UNAVAILABLE` (the `503` of the "
"`closed` failure mode) or `SKIPPED` (the rule did not apply).\n"
"By default only checks slower than `10 ms` are recorded, which shows slow "
"calls to Redis.\n"
"To record all of them, lower the threshold, for example with `jfr configure -"
"-input default.jfc +quarkus.RateLimitEvaluation#threshold=0ms --output rate-"
"limit.jfc`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Neither event records the key or the client address, since recordings are "
"often shared.\n"
"The trace and span IDs are those of xref:jfr.adoc#identifying-requests[the "
"JFR extension]; with OpenTelemetry, they are those of the request's server "
"span."
msgstr ""

msgid "Logs"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"At startup, one INFO line lists the rules; the DEBUG level of the `io."
"quarkus.http.ratelimit` category shows the full configuration of each.\n"
"In dev mode every refusal is logged.\n"
"In other modes refusals are not logged; use the metrics or the JFR events.\n"
"A warning that could repeat on every request, such as a failing key "
"resolver, is logged at most once per `quarkus.http.rate-limit.warning-"
"interval` (one minute by default) for each rule."
msgstr ""

msgid "Customizing rate limiting"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use these Java extension points when the built-in ways of identifying "
"clients or responding to refused requests do not meet your needs."
msgstr ""

msgid "Your own keys"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To count requests some other way, for example by tenant, write a CDI bean "
"that implements `io.quarkus.http.ratelimit.RateLimitKeyResolver`, annotate "
"it with `@Identifier`, and select it with `custom:` followed by the "
"identifier:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`RateLimitContext` exposes the routing context, rule name, phase, and, after "
"authentication, the security identity.\n"
"In this example, authentication must populate the `tenant-id` identity "
"attribute.\n"
"The example fails the lookup when the request is anonymous or the attribute "
"is missing or blank.\n"
"With the default failure handling, these requests receive `503`; they do not "
"receive a fresh allowance under a different key.\n"
"Use HTTP permissions to require authentication when anonymous requests "
"should receive an authentication challenge instead.\n"
"It runs on the event loop, so it must not block, and it must answer within "
"`quarkus.http.rate-limit.key-resolver-timeout` (default `250ms`).\n"
"If it fails or is too slow, `key-resolver-failure=reject` (the default) "
"answers `503`.\n"
"A resolver can deliberately return `Optional.empty()` to exclude a request "
"from this rule entirely.\n"
"Use that only for intentional exemptions; a missing identity or failed "
"lookup should be handled explicitly."
msgstr ""

msgid "Resolver requirements"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Key and limit resolvers are looked up once at startup, so they must be "
"thread-safe."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A blocking call stalls every request on the event loop, and a timeout cannot "
"interrupt it; cache any I/O instead."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Keep the number of distinct keys bounded, and keep personal data out of keys:"
" they are stored, in Redis too, and logged in dev mode."
msgstr ""

msgid "Resolver timeouts"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For each rule, a request spends at most `key-resolver-timeout` in a custom "
"key resolver and `limit-resolver-timeout` in a limit resolver.\n"
"Both default to `250ms`.\n"
"Built-in key resolvers return immediately.\n"
"A resolver that does I/O should bound its own `Uni` with a shorter timeout "
"and decide itself what a timeout means.\n"
"For calls to Redis or another non-local store, see <<store-timeouts>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choosing a fallback key"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The default is to refuse a request whose key cannot be resolved.\n"
"To count these requests by client address instead, explicitly set `quarkus."
"http.rate-limit.key-resolver-failure=ip`.\n"
"This changes who shares the allowance: a tenant with several client "
"addresses can receive several independent IP-based allowances during a "
"lookup failure."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To use one shared fallback allowance instead, catch failures and timeouts "
"yourself and return a fixed key, so that all such requests share one limit:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Changing the response"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To answer refused requests differently, write a CDI bean that implements `io."
"quarkus.http.ratelimit.RateLimitRejectionWriter`.\n"
"It replaces the default writer for all rules, for both exceeded limits "
"(`429`) and lookup or store failures (`503`), and is called after `Retry-"
"After` is set.\n"
"Use `statusCode()` for the response status and `reason()` to distinguish an "
"exceeded limit from a key lookup failure, a limit lookup failure, or an "
"unavailable store.\n"
"`limit()` contains the selected quota when one was resolved; it is empty "
"when key or limit resolution failed.\n"
"It runs on the event loop, must not block, and must set the status and end "
"the response.\n"
"For example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Keep internal rule names, keys, and credentials out of public responses."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reacting to refusals"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every `429` and `503` produced by the rate limiter is also fired as a CDI "
"event of type `io.quarkus.http.ratelimit.RateLimitRejectionInfo`, with the "
"rule, the key, the `Retry-After` and some details of the request.\n"
"Use it to act on a particular key; for counts and dashboards, use the "
"<<metrics>> instead.\n"
"Under attack the event can fire thousands of times per second, so an "
"observer must be cheap."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A `RateLimitRejectionInfo`, which a `RateLimitRejectionWriter` also "
"receives, holds the rule, the phase, the key, the `Retry-After`, the HTTP "
"method, the path the rules were matched against, the client address the `ip` "
"key uses (which may be `null`), and, in the `post-authentication` phase, the "
"user name.\n"
"It also exposes the refusal reason, HTTP status, and selected quota when "
"available.\n"
"`key()` is empty if key resolution failed; `limit()` is empty if key or "
"limit resolution failed.\n"
"It is a snapshot that can be kept after the request ends."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A synchronous observer (`@Observes`) runs on the event loop before the "
"response is written: it must be fast and must not block."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An asynchronous observer (`@ObservesAsync`) runs on a worker thread and may "
"block, but nothing limits how many deliveries are pending: sample or bound "
"expensive work."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A failing observer does not affect the request.\n"
"The key and the path come from the client: escape control characters before "
"logging them, or a CR LF in them starts a forged log line.\n"
"The following observer counts the refusals of each rule and logs one in a "
"thousand:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Writing a store"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A custom store is a CDI bean that implements `io.quarkus.http.ratelimit."
"RateLimitStore`, annotated with `@Identifier(\"<name>\")`, and selected with "
"`store=<name>`.\n"
"The names `local` and `redis` are reserved for the built-in stores.\n"
"A custom store gets the timeout, circuit breaker and failure mode configured "
"for non-local stores, and can also use lease mode.\n"
"It must be thread-safe because multiple event loops can call it concurrently."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "Never block: `tryConsume` runs on the event loop."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Check and consume atomically: of many concurrent calls of cost `1` for a new "
"key, exactly `burst` must be allowed.\n"
"A refusal leaves the state unchanged, except to cap a debt above the burst "
"tolerance."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Take the time from the backend, such as `TIME` in Redis or "
"`clock_timestamp()` in PostgreSQL, not from each instance."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Prefix the key with `policy.id()`: a key is unique only within one rule and "
"phase."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Decide with the policy you are given, which a limit resolver may have "
"selected for the key, and cap a key's debt at `burstToleranceMicros()`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Return `RateLimitDecision.allowed(remaining)` or `RateLimitDecision."
"rejected(retryAfter)`, and signal a failure with a failed `Uni`.\n"
"The factories reject negative remaining permits and nonpositive or "
"unrepresentable retry delays.\n"
"A null result is treated as a failed call."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Treat cost `0` as a read-only check that reports what a cost `1` call would "
"decide."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Keep the state bounded, for example by expiring each key once its burst is "
"full again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Don't touch the backend at startup: the store bean exists even when no rule "
"uses it.\n"
"Connect, create schemas or resolve clients, for example through an "
"`Instance<Pool>`, on the first call."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The decision script of the Redis store, `META-INF/http-rate-limit/try-"
"consume.lua` in the `quarkus-http-rate-limit-redis` jar, is a reference for "
"the arithmetic."
msgstr ""

msgid "Customizing store behaviour"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Most applications never need these.\n"
"They run after the extension's own settings, in priority order: a lower "
"`priority()` runs later and has the last word."
msgstr ""

msgid "Tuning the circuit breaker"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A `io.quarkus.http.ratelimit.RateLimitStoreGuardCustomizer` bean tunes the "
"circuit breaker of each store other than `local`, through the breaker "
"builder of SmallRye Fault Tolerance:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The breaker's delay also sets when the trial call is made and the `Retry-"
"After` of the `closed` failure mode.\n"
"Pool refusals never count as failures, whatever the customizer sets.\n"
"`done()` must not be called.\n"
"The store timeout and the limit of calls in flight cannot be changed, and "
"the `smallrye.faulttolerance.*` configuration does not apply."
msgstr ""

msgid "Observing cache activity"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A `io.quarkus.http.ratelimit.RateLimitLocalStoreCustomizer` bean is called "
"once for each in-memory cache of a rule, the `local` store or the lease "
"cache, and can register statistics of the cache's activity: lookups, "
"updates, and keys forgotten or expired:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Request processing details"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The sections below follow a request through path matching, identification, "
"rule evaluation, and the HTTP response.\n"
"For an introduction to counting clients and choosing an authentication "
"phase, see <<keys>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To limit calls to a Java method rather than HTTP requests, see `@RateLimit` "
"of SmallRye Fault Tolerance, which has no separate limits per client, or the "
"Quarkiverse bucket4j extension."
msgstr ""

msgid "Path and method matching"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`paths` and `methods` accept comma-separated lists.\n"
"If `methods` is omitted, the rule applies to all HTTP methods; method names "
"are normalized to uppercase.\n"
"Unmatched requests proceed without being counted by that rule.\n"
"A request that matches several paths of the same rule is counted only once "
"by that rule."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A path starting with `/` is absolute; a path without a leading `/` is "
"relative to `quarkus.http.root-path`.\n"
"A `*` must be a whole path segment, as in `/a/*/b` or a trailing `/*`, or "
"the build fails.\n"
"With a root path of `/app`, both `api/*` and `/app/api/*` match the "
"application API.\n"
"Relative paths let the same rule work under different root paths.\n"
"Paths are matched as for HTTP permissions.\n"
"A trailing `/*` matches the path itself and its descendants; for example, `/"
"api/*` covers `/api` and `/api/items`.\n"
"Matching uses normalized paths, including removal of matrix parameters, as "
"HTTP permissions do.\n"
"When the router path and the canonical path differ, rules matching either "
"path apply.\n"
"A rule that lists `GET` also applies to `HEAD`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Exempt paths"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The health probes of SmallRye Health (by default `/q/health/live`, `/q/"
"health/ready` and `/q/health/started`) and the Prometheus endpoint of "
"Micrometer (by default `/q/metrics`) are never limited, so that probes and "
"scrapers are never answered `429`.\n"
"Only these exact paths are exempt; `/q/health` itself and health groups are "
"limited like any other path.\n"
"The same paths are exempt from load shedding."
msgstr ""

msgid "Authentication and permissions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When proactive authentication is disabled (`quarkus.http.auth.proactive="
"false`), a `post-authentication` rule authenticates the request for all of "
"its paths, using the mechanisms configured for those paths with xref:"
"security-authorize-web-endpoints-reference.adoc#authorization-using-"
"configuration[HTTP permissions].\n"
"A `post-authentication` rule that applies to every path therefore "
"authenticates every request, and disabling proactive authentication has no "
"effect."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A `post-authentication` rule is matched by the request path, so it "
"authenticates the request before the endpoint has been matched.\n"
"Annotations such as xref:security-authentication-mechanisms.adoc#use-"
"annotations-for-path-based-auth[`@BasicAuthentication`] or xref:security-"
"openid-connect-multitenancy.adoc#annotations-tenant-resolver[`@Tenant`] can "
"select the authentication mechanism or resolve the OIDC tenant only after "
"the endpoint has been matched.\n"
"A request to an endpoint with such an annotation can therefore be rejected "
"with the `401` status, because the rule authenticates it with a different "
"mechanism or tenant than the annotation requires.\n"
"Do not apply a `post-authentication` rule to endpoints that use these "
"annotations.\n"
"Instead, select the authentication mechanism xref:security-authentication-"
"mechanisms.adoc#path-based-authentication[by path] and resolve the OIDC "
"tenant with xref:security-openid-connect-multitenancy.adoc#configure-tenant-"
"paths[tenant paths], or limit these endpoints with a `pre-authentication` "
"rule."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A custom resolver in the `post-authentication` phase is called for anonymous "
"requests too: `RateLimitContext.identity()` tells whether the request is "
"anonymous."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `post-authentication` phase runs before the permission checks, so an "
"anonymous request to a protected path is counted by its IP address before it "
"is answered `401`.\n"
"To refuse anonymous access, use an `authenticated` permission, not a rate "
"limit."
msgstr ""

msgid "Request key representation and memory limits"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`header:<name>` matches the header name case-insensitively and trims the "
"value.\n"
"The value can be a credential, so the key holds its SHA-256 hash, `header:"
"sha256:<hex>`, never the value; a resolver that needs the value reads the "
"header from `RateLimitContext.routingContext()`.\n"
"A hash protects only values that are hard to guess.\n"
"Requests that send the header more than once share one key, `header:"
"repeated`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`principal` uses the user name, which is not qualified by tenant or realm.\n"
"When several authentication mechanisms or OIDC tenants can have users of the "
"same name, use a key resolver that adds the tenant.\n"
"With cookie-based authentication, protect limited paths against cross-site "
"requests, since another site can make a user's browser send them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A key longer than 256 characters, one that starts with `sha256:`, or one "
"that contains `{` or `}` is replaced by `sha256:` and the hex SHA-256 of the "
"key."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each rule keeps at most `max-keys` keys in the memory of an instance.\n"
"When there are more, some are forgotten, and a forgotten key starts again "
"with a full burst.\n"
"Header and principal keys are cheap to vary, so pair such a rule with an "
"`ip` rule.\n"
"A warning is logged when a rule reaches its `max-keys`; raise it unless the "
"cause is a flood."
msgstr ""

msgid "Rule evaluation order"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Within each phase, all matching rules apply, including overlapping paths.\n"
"Rules with inline or named limits are checked by ascending `window`, with "
"ties ordered by rule name.\n"
"Rules using a limit resolver follow, ordered by rule name; their limits are "
"selected only when each rule is evaluated.\n"
"The first refusal stops evaluation.\n"
"Permits consumed by earlier rules are not restored."
msgstr ""

msgid "Combining with load shedding"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Rate limiting is not overload protection.\n"
"It limits each client separately, however idle the application is.\n"
"To protect the application as a whole when it cannot keep up, use xref:load-"
"shedding-reference.adoc[load shedding], which answers `503 Service "
"Unavailable`.\n"
"The two extensions can be used together; load shedding then runs first."
msgstr ""

msgid "Responses"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A client that accepts `application/problem+json`, `application/json` or "
"`text/json` gets an RFC 9457 problem instead of the plain text: `{\"type\":"
"\"about:blank\",\"title\":\"Too Many Requests\",\"status\":429}`.\n"
"Any other client, including one that sends `Accept: */*`, gets the plain "
"text, and a `HEAD` request gets no body.\n"
"The response never says which rule refused the request, or which key was "
"counted."
msgstr ""

msgid "Other status codes"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Besides the `429` described in <<getting-started>>, the extension can answer:"
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`503 Service Unavailable` with `Retry-After`: a non-local store could not "
"decide and the failure mode is `closed`.\n"
"`Retry-After` is chosen at random between one and two circuit breaker delays "
"(5 to 10 seconds by default), so that refused clients don't all come back at "
"once.\n"
"A failed limit lookup without a fallback, or a failed key lookup with the "
"default `key-resolver-failure=reject`, also returns `503`, with a short "
"`Retry-After`.\n"
"The default response uses the same content negotiation as `429`, with the "
"title `Service Unavailable` and status `503`.\n"
"All these refusals use the configured writer and fire a CDI event."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`500 Internal Server Error`: a custom `RateLimitRejectionWriter` failed "
"before ending the response."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The extension sends no headers about the remaining quota on admitted "
"requests: with Redis and lease mode, the remaining quota is only an estimate."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "CORS and request bodies"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Because the rules run after CORS, a `429` carries the CORS headers, and a "
"valid CORS preflight request is answered without being counted.\n"
"For a script to read `Retry-After` from a cross-origin response, list it "
"with `quarkus.http.cors.exposed-headers=Retry-After`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A refused request's unread body is discarded, so that the connection can "
"serve the next request.\n"
"The server reads no more of it than an accepted request may send (`quarkus."
"http.limits.max-body-size`): a larger body is cut off once the response is "
"sent, which over HTTP/1.1 closes the connection, and over HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 "
"ends only the stream of that request.\n"
"If your application installs a body handler for every route, the whole body "
"is read before the limiter runs."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuration reference"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Start with <<getting-started>> for a complete rule, or <<common-tasks>> for "
"examples of adding to it.\n"
"This section explains when configuration changes take effect, how values are "
"validated, and lists every supported property."
msgstr ""

msgid "When configuration takes effect"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The shared defaults `phase`, `request-key`, `store`, `limit-resolver`, and "
"`max-keys` apply to every rule that does not set its own value.\n"
"Set shared defaults directly under `quarkus.http.rate-limit`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An enabled rule needs `paths`, `phase`, `request-key`, and a limit source; "
"`phase` and `request-key` can come from the <<global-settings,shared "
"defaults>>.\n"
"`paths`, `methods`, `phase`, `request-key`, `store` and `limit-resolver` are "
"fixed at build time.\n"
"The others, including the limit itself, are runtime settings: they can be "
"changed when starting the built application, without rebuilding it, but not "
"while it runs.\n"
"Use <<limits,limit resolvers>> for limits that depend on application data "
"and can change between requests.\n"
"See <<configuration-properties>> for every setting and its default."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuration validation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A rule that is invalid, for example one with a key resolver, limit resolver "
"or store that does not exist, fails the build.\n"
"Header-based keys require a valid HTTP header name.\n"
"Ambiguous component selections also fail the build with the configuration "
"property and matching beans; CDI alternatives and default beans follow "
"normal CDI resolution.\n"
"Selections involving runtime lookup conditions (`@LookupIfProperty` or "
"`@LookupUnlessProperty`) are resolved at startup.\n"
"`permits`, `burst`, and `max-keys` must be at least 1, and `window` must be "
"positive.\n"
"The replenishment interval (`window / permits`) must be at least one "
"microsecond, and `interval * burst` must not exceed 30 days.\n"
"`lease-size` requires a non-local store and must be at least 1; `max-"
"overdraft` requires `lease-size` and must be nonnegative.\n"
"For static limits, neither may exceed the burst. For resolved limits, chunks "
"and overdrafts are capped to the selected burst.\n"
"An invalid limit fails startup, and so does a limit for a rule that does not "
"exist, such as a misspelled rule name."
msgstr ""

